WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.782
The inner query groups admissions by patient ID.

00:00:02.782 --> 00:00:05.922
That creates one admission-count row per patient ID.

00:00:05.922 --> 00:00:09.342
The outer query left joins those counts to the patient table.

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:13.387
COALESCE converts missing counts to zero for patients with no admissions.

00:00:13.387 --> 00:00:16.412
This pattern makes the intermediate grain very clear.

00:00:16.412 --> 00:00:20.852
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:20.852 --> 00:00:26.252
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:26.252 --> 00:00:28.377
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:28.377 --> 00:00:33.602
In a real setting such as an e-commerce team finding customers who purchased a target product,

00:00:33.602 --> 00:00:40.312
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:40.312 --> 00:00:41.822
before discussing syntax.

00:00:41.822 --> 00:00:46.297
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
